    {
      "sectionId": "section-disclaimer",
      "heading": "Understanding RPGBOT’s Rating System",
      "sourceAnchor": "disclaimer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "RPGBOT uses a color coding scheme which is simple to understand and easy to read at a glance. This system is designed to help players make informed decisions for their dnd character builds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Red"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Bad, useless options, or options which\n\t\tare extremely situational. Nearly never useful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Orange"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": OK options, or useful options\n\t\tthat only apply in rare circumstances. Useful sometimes."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Green"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Good options. Useful often."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Blue"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Fantastic options, often essential\n\t\tto the function of your character. Useful very frequently."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Feats",
      "sourceAnchor": "feats",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cruel",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Cruel",
      "sourceAnchor": "cruel",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cruel-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The effects are fine, but not amazing, and you don’t get enough dice per day\n\tto justify a feat. If this recharged on a short rest, I would rate it\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cruel-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Note that the damage option is not multiplied on a critical hit. You roll the\n\tdie and add the result to the total rather than adding the die to the\n\tdamage."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-flash-recall",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Flash Recall",
      "sourceAnchor": "flash-recall",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-flash-recall-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Amazing on any prepared spellcaster. The fixed number of prepared spells per\n\tday intentionally limits your versatility, and this makes weird, situational\n\tspells available without the frustration of accumulating a mountain of\n\tscrolls."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-mystic-conflux",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Mystic Conflux",
      "sourceAnchor": "mystic-conflux",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mystic-conflux-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Extremely unpredictable and totally dependent on whatever loot you find. The\n\tability to cast identify is neat, but it’s also a low-level ritual, so it’s\n\tnot very interesting. You’re here for the attunement slot."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-remarkable-recovery",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Remarkable Recovery",
      "sourceAnchor": "remarkable-recovery",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-remarkable-recovery-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A massive improvement to your character’s durability, provided that you have a way to trigger the healing. A Healer’s Kit will automatically stabilize you, which triggers the second bullet’s healing and gets you back to consciousness. If someone hits you with Healing Word instead, you get to add your Con modifier to the amount healed from the spell, potentially giving you another round to live."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-remarkable-recovery-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Several healing options present abuse cases when combined with this feat. It makes Spare the Dying a healing spell with no cost except the action to cast it, provided that you’re at 0 hit points. If you do want to spend a slot, spells which offer repeat healing like Healing Spirit, Aura of Life, and Lay on Hands can repeatedly trigger the third bullet, making those options an extremely efficient source of hit point recovery. Lay on Hands for one point triggers the healing, and you can do that until you run out of points. Regenerate gives you 1+Con hp every turn for an hour. I think the intent is that you get the extra healing once each time something triggers it (once per spell cast, etc.), but talk to your DM."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-remarkable-recovery-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "I would take this before Tough any day of the week. It’s a great option on\n\tfront-line martial characters who are often limited most by running out of hit\n\tpoints and hit dice with which to recover them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-spelldriver",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Spelldriver",
      "sourceAnchor": "spelldriver",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-spelldriver-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This allows for some very powerful combos. It was likely written with spells like Misty Step in mind, and that’s certainly a great example, but that’s certainly not your only option. Clerics can get Spiritual Weapon and Spirit Guardians up and running in one turn."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-spelldriver-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Sorcerers with the Quickened Spell metamagic feat will find this especially\n\tpowerful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-thrown-arms-master",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Thrown Arms Master",
      "sourceAnchor": "thrown-arms-master",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-thrown-arms-master-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This is so close to being good, but it just barely misses the mark because it doesn’t solve the biggest issue with thrown weapons: reloading. You’ll still need Fighting Style (Thrown Weapon Fighting), which means that this is largely only useful for fighters since other classes are unlikely to invest two feats for the novelty of throwing a "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "greataxe",
              "href": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=es9hO7RR6Ig"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-thrown-arms-master-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Once you solve the reloading problem, this is pretty good. A +1 ability score\n\tincrease and likely a big damage boost by switching from throwing javelins to\n\tthrowing greatswords."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-thrown-arms-master-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Extending the range of thrown weapons makes options like daggers much easier to use, but more likely you’re going to throw two-handed weapons for those big damage dice. Lances and greatswords are likely your best choice because two-handed weapons are big enough that your DM might start using encumbrance rules if you’re carrying around a stack of 10-pound mauls."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-thrown-arms-master-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you do use light weapons (daggers, short swords, etc.), the boomerang\n\teffect is a neat novelty. But remember that you’re actively trying to hit, and\n\tyou only get your weapon back on a miss, so you’re doing your best to avoid\n\tbenefiting from the boomerang effect. Consider throwing rapiers instead."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-vital-sacrifice",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Vital Sacrifice",
      "sourceAnchor": "vital-sacrifice",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-vital-sacrifice-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Astoundingly good. 1d6 damage is easily recovered outside of combat with\n\tinexpensive, efficient healing like Prayer of Healing, or you could have\n\tsomeone cast Heroism on you before you start hurting yourself. There doesn’t\n\tappear to be a limit on how many boons you can have, and temporary hit points\n\tseem to protect you just fine."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-vital-sacrifice-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The first two uses for your boon are for martial characters and spell attacks. +1d6 to hit is great for high-damage attacks, such as if you’re using Great Weapon Master or Sharpshooter. The second benefit is great for critical hits, but even if you use it on a regular hit, trading 1d6 damage to yourself for 2d6 to your target is a decent (but not amazing) trade. The phrase “hit with an attack or spell” isn’t correct rules language, but I believe the intent was that it applied to weapon attacks and spell attacks."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-vital-sacrifice-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For casters, -1d6 to a save-or-suck spell is amazing. Stack that with the\n\tpenalty from Mind Sliver (-1d4), and you can reliably use basically whatever\n\tspell you want, including spells which target problematically high\n\tConstitution saves. Hit every enemy that legitimately threatens you with\n\tBlindness and you’ll barely ever need to cast anything above 3rd level."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-vital-sacrifice-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you need a nerf for this, limit the user to having one boon at a time.\n\tEven with the small hp cost to use it, the action economy cost to get a new\n\tboon mid-combat will prevent things from getting too crazy."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
